# MOFs and COFs for Batteries and Supercapacitors

Xing Gao1 · Yu Dong1 $\cdot\cdot$ Siwu Li1 $\cdot$ Junwen Zhou1 $\cdot$ Lu Wang1 $\cdot$ Bo Wang1
Abstract
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) as two burgeoning families of crystalline porous materials (CPMs) have been extensively investigated and applied in various fields on account of their enticing features as large surface area, controllable crystalline structure and highly ordered pores/channels. However, the greatest stumbling block to their widespread application in electrochemical energy storage systems is the inferior electrical conductivity. Therefore, numerous efforts have been exerted on exploiting the advantages and remedying the disadvantages. In this review, we mainly focus on pristine MOFs and COFs, emphasize the recent progress and highlight the milestones of their applications in the fields of lithium-ion batteries, lithium–sulfur batteries, lithium–air batteries and supercapacitors. We hope to provide a constructive view of the structure–activity relationship between CPMs and energy storage systems and promote their future development.
Keywords  MOFs $\cdot\cdot$ COFs $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Li-ion batteries $\cdot\cdot$ Li–S batteries $\cdot\cdot$ Li–air batteries $\cdot\cdot$ Supercapacitors
## 1  Introduction
Crystalline porous materials (CPMs) [1–4], including metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [5–10] and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [11–15], are a class of porous solids with appealing properties such as large surface area, well-defined crystalline structure and highly ordered pores/ channels. More specifically, MOFs, including a subfamily of zeolitic-imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) [6, 16, 17], are constructed by metal nodes (secondary building units, SBUs) and organic linkers. COFs are ordered structures built up from organic building blocks via covalent bonds. CPMs have attracted much attention in sensor, gas storage/separation, catalysis and energy storage [18–32].
With the rapid development of modern society, the more dependence we have on energy, the more severe the energy crisis will be. The polluted air, contaminated water and increasing population all drive us to pursue renewable resources, such as wind and solar energy [33–35]. Thereafter, reliable electrochemical energy storage (EES) system is highly demanded. The earlier EES technologies, including batteries with high energy density and supercapacitors with high power density, have already been applied in people’s daily lives [36–41]. However, with the continuous upgrade of electronics and electric vehicles (EVs), we need to improve their performances and develop new techniques to meet higher requirements [42]. Besides the well-known lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), lithium–sulfur batteries (LSBs) and lithium–air batteries (LABs) are widely studied as new promising candidates [43–53]. These batteries exhibit high theoretical energy density and specific capacity as well as low cost [54]. Nevertheless, there are still many challenges which dampen their practical application, such as the poor conductivity of active materials (sulfur) and discharge products $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3},$ which cause low utilization of sulfur, inferior cycling stability, the “shuttle effect” in LSBs and significant volume variation during cycling that further reduces the battery cycle life [55–59]. Unlike the traditional porous materials such as zeolite or active carbon, MOFs and COFs not only have permanent porosity, but also have tunable crystalline structure [1, 9, 13, 15, 60]. Therefore, more precise design and control at atomic level could be achieved in CPMs. These advantages provide us a multifunctional platform to solve the above-mentioned issues of EES.
Up to now, MOFs and COFs have been extensively studied and exhibit huge potential in EES systems in various ways, such as directly being utilized as active materials [61, 62] and employed as templates and precursors to obtain derived porous carbon/metal oxides/metal sulfide [56, 63–67]. Numerous studies have been published in the past decades, and several previous reviews have summarized the applications of MOFs and their derivatives in electrochemistry fields [68–75]. In this review, we mainly focus on the application of pristine CPMs in rechargeable batteries and supercapacitors (Fig. 1). Specifically, we first review the progress of pristine CPMs in cathode materials, anode materials, modified separators and solid electrolytes for LIBs, followed by the development in LSBs and LABs, and ended up with the research in supercapacitors. We aim to summarize the pioneering works, highlight the function of pristine CPMs in EES systems and advance their further development.
## 2  Lithium‑Ion Batteries
Myriad energy storage technologies have been proposed with the increasing demand in energy storage for mobile devices and EVs. LIBs have attracted intense research efforts owing to their advantages of higher voltage, higher energy density and longer cycle life than the traditional lead-acid and Ni-based batteries and have been widely used as the convenient power source for portable electronic devices since Sony first introduced commercial LIBs into our life in 1991 [42, 54]. With the gradual deepening study, researchers have developed a series of electrode materials, for example, $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}(\mathrm{LCO})$ , $\mathrm{LiFePO_{4}(L F P)}$ and $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}C o_{0.2}M n_{0.3}O_{2}}$ (NCM) as cathode materials [76–80]. Anode materials are mainly carbon materials and $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ [81–84]. However, the energy density, cycle life and rate performance of LIBs still need to be further improved to meet the increasing requirement for energy. For cathode materials, layered metal oxides could be unstable and tend to occur irreversible phase change during cycling, resulting in a rapid decline of capacity. Besides, the lack of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion paths and high potential barrier for redox reaction lead to an incomplete lithiation/delithiation process [64]. Anode materials are also facing the above issues. Moreover, silicon as another promising anode material has severe expansion problems, hindering its application in LIBs [85, 86]. Solid electrolyte interface (SEI) layer forms on the surface of anode materials during cycling, which can effectively protect the anode. However, the naturally formed SEI layer is not stable enough because it will rupture and reform repeatedly during cycling, consuming a large amount of lithium and electrolyte [87]. This poor conductor of electrons and ions also impairs the activity of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion in the material.
CPMs have been investigated as electrode materials for LIBs since 2006 and 2007 when Chen et al. first used MOF177 as the anode material [88] and Tarascon et al. utilized MIL-53 as the cathode material [89], respectively. Inspired by these pioneering works, CPMs are considered as attractive candidates in LIBs because of their low cost, high stability, porosity and easy preparation.
## 2.1  Cathode Materials
In 2007, Tarascon et  al. firstly proposed MOFs as the rechargeable intercalation cathode materials [89]. $\mathrm{[Fe^{HI}(O H)_{0.8}F_{0.2}(O_{2}C C_{6}H_{4}C O_{2})]\cdot H_{2}O}$ $({\mathrm{Fe}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}})$ , a mixed-valence state Fe-based MOF, showed a reversible capacity of $70\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $140\mathrm{mAhL^{-1}}$ within the voltage range of $1.5–3.5\mathrm{~V~}$ at $\mathrm{C}/40$ , which corresponded to the insertion/deinsertion of $0.6\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ per unit formula concomitant with the reversible redox between $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ (Fig. 2a, b). The result of potentiostatic intermittent titration techniques indicated two different domains: For the lithiation number below 0.35, the current curves followed a Cottrell-type law indicative of a solid-solution insertion process that is kinetically limited by ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion, a more limited diffusion step which linked to the two-phase transition in high lithiation number. Mössbauer spectra further evidenced the tions between the host quinone molecules and the MOF linkers. Voltage composition profiles are shown in d for Li/MIL-53(Fe)_vac, Li/ quinone, and Li/MIL-53(Fe)_quinone Swagelok cells cycled in the range $1.8\mathrm{-}3.5\mathrm{~V~}$ at a C/10 rate, respectively. Reprinted with permission from Ref. [90]. e The crystal structure of MIL-68(Fe). Top view along the channel axis. f Redox potential $\mathrm{(Li/Li^{+})}$ versus Li composition of an MIL-68(Fe) electrode cycled in a galvanostatic mode at C/10 between 1.5 and $3.5{\mathrm{~V}}.$ Reprinted with permission from Ref. [91]
reversibility of MIL-53 during the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ insertion/deinsertion. Later in 2009 [90], Tarascon et al. encapsulated the electroactive 1,4-benzoquinone molecule within the channels of MIL-53 and then used X-ray diffraction (XRD), differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) and magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (MAS NMR) to thoroughly characterize the structure. After heated, a new phase with 0.5 quinone molecules/MIL-53 formula unit was formed (Fig.  2c, d). The 1,4-benzoquinone helped enhance the electrochemical capacity; however, the capacity retention was poor due to the progressive exchange between quinone and electrolyte (dimethyl carbonate) molecules. Serre et al. synthesized a new porous Fe-based MOF, MIL-68 [91], and studied its behavior as the cathode material for LIBs. The fact is that the inorganic SBUs of MIL-53 and MIL-68 are same but MIL-68 owns larger and rigid pores which might be the reason of a lower capacity observed with MIL-68 (Fig. 2e, f). The electrolyte is unable to enter these triangular-shaped pores, so the diffusion of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ is more difficult. Meng et al. reported the electrochemical performance of MIL-101(Fe) for the first time in 2015 [92]. After lithiation, MIL-101 hosts $0.62\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}$ which is similar to that of MIL-53 (0.6) and higher than that of MIL-68 (0.35). A high cutoff voltage of $2.0\mathrm{V}$ was achieved, but the capacity retention was pretty poor that it can only remain $0.37\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}$ after 5 cycles. It was found that the redox chemistry, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ was incompletely reversible and DMC (dimethyl carbonate) tended to replace ­Cl–ions to reduce the free energy of Fe ion reduction. The authors proposed the significance of effective strategies to stabilize the SBU and improve the reversibility of the redox in MIL-101. Yamada et al. also investigated MIL-101 as the cathode materials for LIBs [93]. The deepest discharge $(\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}=0.7\$ ) among all reported MOF-based cathodes at that time was obtained, and it remained about $0.62\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}$ after 100 cycles. Different from the Meng’s work, this work expanded the charge voltage to $4.3\mathrm{V}$ which greatly enhanced the reversibility of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . When comparing the capacity, MIL-101 can rival against commercial LCO or LFP. In addition to the Fe-based monometallic MOFs, a lot of Fe-based mixed-metal MOFs also have been applied as cathode materials in LIBs, such as Fe-CN-Mn framework [94], CuFe-PBA $\mathrm{\PBA=}$ Prussian blue analogues) [95, 96], MnFe-PBA [97], NiFe-PBA [98].
Awaga et al. obtained a new MOF [61], named $\mathrm{Cu}(2,7-$ AQDC) $(2,7-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AQDC}=2,7-$ -anthraquinonedicarboxylic acid), with redox-active sites on both metal clusters $(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Ac})_{4})$ and ligands. This MOF exhibited a specific capacity of $147\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is very close to its theoretical capacity of $\sim162\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (3 electrons per formula of $\mathrm{Cu}(2,7{\cdot}\mathrm{AQDC}),$ ), but it suffers from a visible capacity loss of ${\sim}40\ \mathrm{mA}\ h\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles which was primarily due to the inadequate extraction of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ during charging (Fig. 3a, b). Thereafter, in 2016, Awaga et al. replaced the previous $\mathbf{Cu-MOF}$ with $\mathrm{Mn}_{7}(2,7{\cdot}\mathrm{AQDC})_{6}(2,6{\cdot}\mathrm{AQDC})$ $(\mathrm{DMA})_{6}$ $(\mathrm{DMA}=N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylacetamide) [99] and studied the electrochemical properties of this Mn-MOF. A new mechanism, named as “bipolar charging,” was revealed in this solid-state electrochemical reaction. In this mechanism, $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and anions $\mathrm{PF}^{6-}$ all insert into the channels of MOFs and contribute to the capacity (Fig. 3c, e). Mn-MOF-based battery exhibited a high initial capacity of $205\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ and almost no capacity loss as well as a nearly $100\%$ coulombic efficiency (CE) within 50 cycles, which is higher than $\mathbf{Cu-MOF}$ $(\sim70\%$ in 50 cycles) (Fig. 3d). Moreover, $\mathrm{Cu}$ -TCA ­ $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{TCA}=$ tricarboxytriphenyl amine) [100], MOPOF (metal organic-phosphate open frameworks) [101], MIL-47(V) [102], and MIL-136 [103] were also explored as cathode materials in LIBs.
Organic materials with redox-active sites are also considered as promising candidates for LIBs by virtue of light weight (metal-free), adequate resources and so on. But they are still facing with drawbacks such as low electrical conductivity, fast capacity fading and low efficiency. COFs as the ordered porous organic materials can provide robust carbon network in favor of cycling stability and abundant channels for the transport of electrons and lithium ions. Jiang et al. reported a mesoporous COF $(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{TP}^{-}}\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{NCI}^{-}}\mathrm{COF})$ on carbon nanotube (CNT) as the cathode material for energy storage (Fig. 4a, b) [104]. The initial capacity of this COF was about $67~\mathrm{\mA~h~g^{-1}}$ , and it can maintain stable after 700 cycles at the rate of $2.4\:\mathrm{C}$ . However, the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction of 2D layers usually cause close stacking in an eclipsed fashion in COFs, and the long ion transportation path and insufficiently exposed redox-active sites significantly impede its capacity and rate performance. Our group synthesized and exfoliated three 2D COFs (DAAQ-TFP-COF, DABQ-TFP-COF, and TEMPO-COF) $(\mathrm{DAAQ}=2,6$ -diaminoanthraquinone, $\mathrm{TFP}=1,3,5$ -tris(4- formylphenyl)benzene, $\mathrm{DABQ}=2,5.$ -diamino-1,4-benzonquinone, $\mathrm{TEMPO}=2,2,6,6$ -tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl) into few-layer nanosheets (ECOFs) (Fig. 4c, d) [18]. The ion/electron migration length and diffusion speed in ECOFs were shortened and accelerated, respectively, and more redox-active sites for electrochemical reaction were exposed after delamination. These all lead to enhanced capacity and rate performance (Fig. 4e). In particular, DAAQ-ECOF showed a high capacity of $145~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and negligible $2\%$ capacity loss after 1800 cycles at high current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ (Fig. 4f), and the high reversibility of quinone groups on DAAQ-ECOF is greatly benefit for the remarkable CE (nearly $99\%$ during cycling). When using a smaller structural unit, DABQ, to replace DAAQ in DAAQ-TFP-COF, the COF presented a $0.5–\mathrm{V}$ higher discharge voltage than that of DAAQ-ECOF owing to the electronic effect and performed a higher discharge capacity of $210~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}(\mathrm{Fig.4g})$ . The exfoliation strategy greatly tapped the potential of COFs for LIBs.
## 2.2  Anode Materials
Chen et al. first reported a MOF as the electrode material for LIBs in 2006 [88]. They synthesized MOF-177, a new MOF with unit formula of $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(1,3,5\$ -benzenetribenzoate) $)_{2}$ and found that MOF-177 displayed a high initial capacity of $400~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ but it rapidly faded to $105~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in the following cycles (Fig. 5a). The structure of MOF-177 had been destroyed after discharge, which was evidenced by TEM images (Fig. 5b, c) and XPS results (Fig. 5d). Thus, the reaction process of the first cycle [equations of (1) and (2)] is different from the other cycles [Eq. (3)].
$$
\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O(BTB)}_{2}(\mathrm{DEF})_{m}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{n}+e+\mathrm{Li}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{Zn}+\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}
