Another $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF [105], $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}$ , with diamondoid structure was also applied as the anode material and achieved a reversible capacity of $560\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 60 cycles at $60\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . The structure of this MOF can be regenerated during the cycling because of the reversible reaction of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ with $\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}$ ligands.
Zhao et al. synthesized a Zn-MOF-Crown as the anode material for LIBs (Fig. 5e) [62]. During the cycling, ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ mainly interacted with the organic linker, which contains a crown ether unit, keeping the MOF skeleton intact, which greatly enhanced the cyclability of LIBs and realized only $12\%$ capacity loss after 500 cycles. These promising results confirmed that the ingenious design of MOFs could effectively enhance the battery performance. Fluorine is the most electronegative element and can strongly interact with ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ . Thus, Yang et al. doped F into a Mn-based MOF to partially replace oxygen sites, named F-MOF-3 [106], which significantly improved the electrochemical performance of LIBs (Fig. 5f). F-MOF-3 exhibited a reversible capacity of $927\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ compared with $30\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of pristine MOF electrode (Fig. 5g) and even maintained $494\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 500 cycles at high current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The superior performance of F-MOF-3 electrode is mainly because of F doping that made metal centers become more stable and created more active sites to promote the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion as well as protected the surface from corrosion of HF. The possible mechanism of F-MOF-3 reacting with ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ can be expressed as Fig. 5h. Tang et al. introduced carboxyl groups on the linkers of ZIF-67 through post-synthetic modification (from $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ to –COOH) and deposited Ag nanoparticles on the surface of ZIF-67 to enhance the conductivity (Fig. 5i) [107]. Capacity of $800~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $0.28~\mathrm{C}$ after 100 cycles and a CE of $99\%$ were obtained from the modified ZIF-67.
Stein et al. chose UiO-66 constructed by $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}^{12+}}$ cluster nodes with terephthalate ligands as the anode material for LIBs [108] and studied the effect of MOFs particle size on battery performance. The average particle size of UiO-66 was adjusted by altering the synthesis temperature (Fig. 6a). The UiO-66 with the smallest particle size exhibited the highest capacity retention $\mathrm{118\mA\h\g^{-1}}$ at $0.2\mathrm{C})$ than the other three MOF samples due to the shorten ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion path and the increased contact area with the conductor (Fig. 6b). Sub-5-nm ultrasmall MOF nanocrystals were also synthesized to be encapsulated within N-doped carbon/graphene as anode materials [109], which delivered reversible capacities of $1301~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $494~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ and $40\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Co-based MOF nanowires (CoCOP nanowires) demonstrated a reversible capacity of $1107~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $20\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ and a ultralong cycle life of 1000 cycles at 1 C [110]. It was greatly benefited from the robust framework and 1D MOF nanowires that supplied interpenetrating channels for ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport. 2D MOFs nanosheets also exhibited appreciable performance in LIBs, such as S–Co-MOF [111], u-CoTDA $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TDA}=2,5$ -thiophenedicarboxylic) [112], H–Co-MOF [113], and MnUMOFNs nanosheets [114]. 2D MOFs nanosheets can significantly diminish ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport distance and expose more active sites for electrochemical reactions. Mn-UMOFNs nanosheets delivered a high capacity of $1187\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , which was very close to its theoretical capacity ( $(1392\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ), and retained $701\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at high current density of $2\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ .Hu et al. prepared CoBTC $\mathrm{(BTC=1}$ , 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid) with various morphologies and dimensions through varying different solvents [ethanol (EtOH), N, $N_{\mathbf{\delta}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), EtOH/DMF (1:1, $\mathrm{vol}\%$ )] (Fig. 6c) [115]. The morphology is affected by the solvent according to Fig. 6d: hollow microspheres were formed in EtOH solvent (CoBTC-EtOH), but a layered structure crystallized in DMF (CoBTC-DMF), and a more apparent layered morphology generated in mixed solvent (CoBTC-DMF/EtOH). The hollow CoBTC-EtOH exhibited a reversible capacity of $473\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at the current density of $2\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ after 500 cycles (Fig. 6e). Therefore, both reducing the size of MOFs particles to nanometer scale and reducing the dimension of MOFs structures are indeed promising routes to enhance their electrochemical performances.
COFs have also been used as anode materials for LIBs. Liu et  al. synthesized conductive 2D COF nanosheets through in situ chemical oxidative polymerization on $\mathrm{cu}$ foil [116]. This 2D COF exhibited a high specific capacity of $666\mathrm{{mAhg^{-1}}}$ due to the porous nanosheets that can hold abundant electrolyte and shorten the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ diffusion distance. Zhao et al. developed two COFs that not only exhibited superior gas selectivity but also showed an considerable ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ storage ability (about $600~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 500 cycles) [117]. Besides, a novel polyoxometalate-based MOF (NNU-11) was also employed as the anode material and demonstrated that $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction is beneficial to LIBs properties [118].
## 2.3  Separator
The separator also plays a vital role in LIBs in preventing internal short circuit and providing ion-conducting channels. CPMs can act as the separator independently or as a component in the separator in LIBs [119, 120]. Wang et al. proposed a Ti-based MOF $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{MIL-}125, $ ) coated on the commercial polypropylene (PP) separator to enhance the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport and suppress the growth of dendrites on the lithium metal electrode [121]. The Li|Li symmetric batteries using MOF-coated separator can cycle for more than $1200\mathrm{~h~}$ at $1~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}/1~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ without short circuit and achieves an average CE of $98.5\%$ within the initial 200 cycles. It was proved that the superior electrochemical stability is attributed to the amine functional group in MOFs based on the results of the cycling performance of Li|Cu cells. Amine groups can strongly interact with ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ because of its electronegativity and promote $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ transport. Another strategy is to coat MOF on the surface of electrode materials directly. Shen et al. directly coated ZIF-67 onto the surface of $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}C o_{0.2}M n_{0.3}O_{2}}$ electrode as the separator [122]. This ZIF-67-based separator showed good wettability, substantial electrolyte uptake capacity and great thermal stability. Compared with the PP separator, this functional separator delivered a better rate performance and a higher initial discharge capacity of $142\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.2\mathrm{C}$ . It also delivered great performance in long-time cycling and high temperature (55 and $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ battery test. And this MOF-based separator can effectively promote the connection of active materials with the separator and reduce the interfacial resistance.
Lithium metal is considered as the most promising anode for the next-generation batteries on account of the high theoretical capacity ( $3860\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ and the lowest potential $(-3.04\mathrm{V})$ . However, one of the critical defects that hamper its application is the inevitable dendritic Li growth during cycling, which can cause serious safety problems. In 2018, Zhou et al. reported a MOF-modified electrolyte in which ion transport was ideally controlled and homogeneous Li electrodeposition was formed without Li dendrites (Fig. 7a)
[123]. HKUST-1, with an $8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ window size channel for ionic transport, effectively restricted the anion $(\mathrm{TFSI}^{-}$ , anion of the lithium (bis)trifluoromethanesulfonylimide salt, $7.9\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) migration but achieved a rapid ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ flux and homogeneous Li electrodeposition on the surface of anode (Fig. 7b). The symmetric cell with MOF-modified electrolyte exhibited a low overpotential and long cycle life over $800\mathrm{~h~}$ without short circuit. From Fig. 7c, d, obvious Li dendrites formed on the Li metal after cycling when using the pristine electrolyte, while after using this MOF-modified electrolyte, there was no dendrite, directly supporting that MOF can successfully suppress the dendritic Li formation. In $\mathrm{LilLi_{4}T i_{5}O_{12}}$ batteries test, the MOF-modified electrolyte exhibited no capacity decay after 2000 cycles at 5 C (Fig. 7e).
## 2.4  Solid and Modified Electrolytes
Conventional LIBs are generally utilizing liquid organic electrolytes; however, the inferior energy density and the formidable safety issue substantially obstruct its application in the scenario of requiring high power output and fast charge/ discharge. Lithium solid-state batteries (LSSBs) are considered to be a hopeful candidate for next generation of energy storage devices.
Theoretically, highly porous MOFs can host guest electrolyte, guarantee effective transport of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ and control Li deposition. Poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO)-based polymer electrolyte has been widely studied since 1976 [124]. Liu et al. reported nano-MOF-5 as the filler for PEO-based solid-state electrolyte [125]. PEO with molecular weight of ${\sim}4,000{,}000$ was mixed with L $\mathrm{\DeltaN}({\mathrm{SO}}_{2}{\mathrm{CF}}_{3})_{2}$ (LiTFSI) and MOF-5 to form the composite polymer electrolyte (CPE) which showed an ionic conductivity of $3.16\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ when the weight ratio of MOF-5 to PEO-LiTFSI equals to $10~\mathrm{wt}\%$ . The enhanced ionic conductivity is owing to the unsaturated metal sites on the surface of MOF-5 which interact with LiTFSI and the channels in the MOF-5 which provide ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport route (Fig. 8a). From the result of Fig. 8b, c, MOF-5 not only strongly adsorbed electrolyte but also trapped trace impurities in solvent which prevented the formation of passivation layer on lithium metal. In $\mathrm{LiFePO_{4}}/$ Li solid battery test at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $10\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ -MOF-5/PEOLiTFSI presented a higher capacity of $143~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and a smaller overpotential even at high current density compared to PEO-LiTFSI. It also exhibited superior behavior in higher-temperature test $(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Subsequently, Al-BTC/ PEO-LiTFSI [126], Al-MIL-53/PEO-LiTFSI [127], CuBDC/PEO-LiTFSI $(\mathrm{BDC}=1,4$ -dicarboxybenzene) [128], Al-TPA/PEO-LiTFSI ( $\mathrm{{TPA}=}$ terephthalic acid) [129], NiBTC/PEO-LiTFSI [130] and cationic MOFs [131] also have been successfully designed as CPE for solid-state batteries and all achieved excellent cycling performances. These progresses are mainly attributed to the high ionic conductivity, thermal stability and low interfacial resistances by introducing MOFs in the CPE.
Pan et  al. successfully created an effective 3D conductive solid-like electrolyte (SLE) which combined the advantages of MOFs and Li-ionic liquid (Li-IL) (Fig. 8d) [132]. This SLE demonstrated a high ionic conductivity of $3.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at room temperature, an excellent ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ transfer number of 0.36, a wide working temperature range $\cdot-20$ to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and a reversible capacity of $145~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{C}$ at both $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 8e). Moreover, this battery achieved super-high active materials loading of $25~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . The high ionic conductivity and nanowetted interface of Li-IL $@$ MOF SLE that guaranteed the close contact of SLE and active electrodes are the principle reasons for the superb performance. Dunn et al. utilized open metal sites in MOFs skeletons to anchor anions of electrolytes and obtained a series of pseudo-solid-state electrolytes which have excellent ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ conductivity of $10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ and low activation energy. Pan et al. also introduced $Z\mathrm{r}$ -UiO-67 into the garnet $\mathrm{Li}_{7}\mathrm{La}_{3}\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ (LLZO) solid-state electrolyte to reduce the interfacial resistance and accelerate $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ transport [133]. Benefiting from MOFs, the established SSE displayed a high ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ conductivity of $10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ and good stability for LFP and LCO.
## 2.5  Summary
It has been over a decade from the year when the first MOF, MOF-177, was used as the active material for LIBs (in 2006), and the potential of CPMs has been gradually tapped out. Except as electrode active materials (Table 1), MOFs also can act as separators and fillers for SSB (Table 2) and so on.
As shown in Fig. 9, there are many advantages of CPMs in LIBs, such as porosity, designability, stability and good compatibility (as filler for SSB). The ordered channels can facilitate ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion. Abundant redox-active sites and excellent interfacial charge transfer can largely reduce the Li-binding energy and the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport resistance. But there still exist challenges. The direction of future development of
CPMs in the LIBs might be achieving nanoparticle size and unique morphology (i.e., 1D nanowires and 2D nanosheets), solving conductivity issues as well as exploiting bimetallic MOFs and ligand modification strategies. There is no doubt that CPMs will be even more extensively studied in LIBs in the future and its practical application will be greatly accelerated.
## 3  Lithium–Sulfur Batteries
Lithium–sulfur batteries are commonly considered as one of the most competitive candidates for next-generation batteries due to their high theoretical capacity $(\sim1672\mathrm{mAhg^{-1})}$ and energy density $(\sim2567~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}},$ , much greater than that of the commercialized electrode materials of LIBs (about 3–5 times). More than that, the benefits such as abundant resource, low cost and non-toxicity are also pretty enticing [54]. However, although with the above advantages, the LSBs have not replaced the existing energy systems yet, owing to the following major drawbacks: (1) the large gaps of capacity and energy between the theoretical and practical values and (2) the low CE and the rapid capacity decay. Compared with conventional LIBs, the mechanism of LSBs is quite different. The overall redox reaction can be described by the reaction of S (insoluble) ${\bf\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x}$ (soluble) ${\bf\leftrightarrow}\mathrm{Li}_{2}\bf S$ (insoluble); thus, it undergoes a dissolution–deposition route through soluble polysulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x})$ which can dissolve into the electrolyte and freely diffuse between the cathode and anode to launch a so-called shuttle effect (Fig. 10) [134]. The polysulfides reach to the anode and react with the metallic lithium and then form insoluble discharge products $\mathrm{(Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ ) on the surface of anode, causing a low utilization of the active materials and fast attenuation of capacity [45, 135, 136]. Therefore, on this way, we need to reduce the content of sulfur in the electrode due to the electrical insulating nature of insoluble discharge products and the active material S (electrical conductivity: $5\times10^{-30}\mathrm{{S}\thinspace{c m}^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) to maximize the S utilization. (3) An $80\%$ volume change occurs during cycling, which can cause poor contact between sulfur and conductive agents (porous carbon), even destroy the electrode structure in the end [37, 44].
To date, many studies are focusing on how to prevent polysulfide dissolving and shuttling during cycling. Carbonaceous materials, such as carbon cloth, porous carbon, carbon nanotubes, spheres, fibers and MOFs derivatives [137–145], have been greatly investigated as effective sulfur hosts due to their high electronic conductivity and strong adsorption/ absorption abilities to polysulfides. Using carbon, polymers and two-dimensional materials as functional separators have also been considered as an effective approach to enhance the LSBs’ performance because their high surface area and porosity can prevent the shuttling [146–148].
CPMs are of great interest in LSBs owing to their extraordinary porosity, structural diversity, tunable cavity size and so on. Therefore, CPMs are broadly explored as novel host materials and separators in LSBs to enhance the battery performance.
## 3.1  MOFs as Host Materials
As the host material of sulfur, MOFs with abundant porosity, high specific surface area and strong chemical bonds can provide not only physical but also chemical affinities with sulfur and polysulfides within the MOFs scaffolds and therefore enhance the specific capacity, cycle life and CE.
Tarascon, Férey and co-workers pioneered in using a mesoporous $\mathrm{Cr}$ -based MOF (MIL-100(Cr)) as the host material to impregnate sulfur in LSBs in 2011 [149]. After a melt-diffusion process, $48\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ of sulfur was infiltrated in the MIL-100(Cr) and formed MIL- $100(\mathrm{Cr})/\mathrm{S}@155$ which exhibited a notable enhancement in the capacity retention (Fig. 11a). This can be ascribed to the chemical interaction between polysulfides and the oxygenated framework.
