Li et al. investigated four different MOFs: ZIF-8 with small cage-type pores, HKUST-1 with Lewis acidic sites, MIL-53 with breathing networks and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 with Lewis basic groups, as well as the size effect of ZIF-8 for LSBs (Fig. 12a) [155]. ZIF-8 with the smallest window size $(3.4\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and a uniform nanoparticle size ( $100{-}200\mathrm{nm}$ ) exhibited the exceptional performance (Fig. 12b–e), indicating that the small aperture can more strongly trap polysulfides and the smaller particle size is beneficial to the internal $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ transport. Subsequently in 2015, Li et al. systematically reported the influence of the hosts’ particle size on the LSBs’ performance [156]. ZIF-8 samples with various particle sizes from $20\mathrm{nm}$ to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were heated with sulfur at $155^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to get corresponding ${\cal S}^{\varpi}{\cal Z}\mathrm{IF}{-}8 $ composites as $\mathbf{S}@$ MOF cathodes (Fig. 12f). The battery performance revealed that smaller particle size, higher utilization of sulfur. The optimal performance (over $950\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{C}$ ) was achieved when the particle size of ZIF-8 was smaller than $20\mathrm{nm}$ ; however, a moderate size ${\sim}200\mathrm{nm})$ can deliver the best capacity retention. This could be attributed to the overly high external surface area, which allows soluble polysulfides more easily escape from the ZIF-8 nanoparticles (Fig. 12g, h). Therefore, the host material with “golden size” ${\sim}200\mathrm{nm}$ for ZIF-8) should lead to a high capacity as well as an excellent capacity retention at the same time.
There are three criteria for a qualified host material in LSBs: polarity, porosity and conductivity. MOFs have already been proved great potential to be hosts for active materials [157–159]; however, it is hard for MOFs to achieve these three criteria simultaneously due to their poor conductivity (Fig. 13a). The inferior conductivity of MOF-based LSBs usually results in poor utilization of active materials and sluggish electrochemical redox kinetics of sulfur. Consequently, combining MOF/S composite with conductive materials, such as carbon nanotubes [146, 160] and polymers (i.e., polypyrrole (ppy) [157], poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) (PEDOT) [161]) and loading MOFs on a conductive substrate, for example carbon cloth [162], are two feasible ways to enhance the exploitation of sulfur and alleviate the above issues. A representative ppy-MOF/S cathode was achieved by Deng et al. [157]. The electrical conductivity of the pristine MOFs was low (about $10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{m}^{-1}.$ ), but after introducing the conductive polypyrrole, the conductivity was improved dramatically $(3.1\times10^{1}$ , $1.2\times10^{2}$ and $6.7\:\mathrm{S\:m^{-1}}$ for ppy-MIL-101/S, ppyMIL-53/S and ppy-PCN-224/S, respectively) (Fig. 13a). The ppy-PCN-224/S electrode with cross-linked pores and channels showed a high capacity of 670 and $440\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at 10 C after 200 and 1000 cycles, respectively. Three MOFs with different pore shapes (1D channels in MIL-53, 3D hierarchical nanocages for MIL-101 and cross-linked pore and channels for PCN-224) were thoroughly investigated (Fig. 13b). Shortened ion diffusion route and enlarged pore width are able to accommodate the transport of more ions (Fig. 13c). Among the three MOFs, PCN-224 provides the shortest ions diffusion pathways and the largest pore aperture $(19.3\mathring\mathrm{A})$ . However, MIL-101 that delivers the longest diffusion pathway and smallest pore aperture exhibited the lowest electrochemical performance at 5 C. Except for conductive polymers, carbon materials interpenetrating through MOFs can also give a conductive and stable electrode (Fig. 13d) [146]. The carbon nanotubes (CNTs)-interpenetrated MOFs thin film is a binder-free, flexible electrode for LSBs and displayed a high volumetric energy density and a long cycle life (Fig. 13e).
## 3.2  MOFs as Separators
As the separator for LSBs, MOFs are always playing a crucial role as ionic sieves by providing ordered pores and adjustable chemical structures. Zhou et al. reported a novel and effective MOF-199-based separator $(\mathrm{MOF}@ $ GO) ( $\mathrm{{GO}=}$ graphene oxide) which can selectively sieve ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions and block soluble polysulfides [163]. MOF-199 owns a small pore size of $9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , which is significantly smaller than
