polysulfides $\mathrm{(Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{x}$ , $4<x\leqslant8)$ ; thus, the separator is able to block polysulfides (Fig. 14a). The polysulfides permeation experiment showed that the MOF@GO separator can successfully suppress the diffusion of polysulfides within $48\mathrm{h}$ , while the common separator was unable to block soluble polysulfides in the absence of MOFs (Fig. 14b). Afterward, Zhou et al. proposed another kind of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF-based separator for LSBs [164]. The thus-obtained battery with the ionic sieve-liked separator demonstrated a slight capacity decay of $0.041\%$ per cycle and an average $98.8\%$ CE at $1\mathrm{C}$ over 1000 cycles (Fig. 14c).
Li et al. performed a comparative study with several MOFs (Y-FTZB, ZIF-7, ZIF-8, and HKUST-1) films, which have various pore sizes and chemical structures [165]. However, the unavoidable grain boundaries in separators may allow the soluble polysulfides diffuse from cathode area to Li anode area. Interestingly, the authors unearthed that the packing morphology of MOF particles has important influence on minimizing the shuttle effects. The Y-FTZBbased layer showed a seamless film structure, where small particles filled in the boundary space of large particles (Fig. 14d). Y-FTZB-based separator achieved the highest initial capacity $(1101\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}},$ ) compared with HKUST-1, ZIF-7, ZIF-8 and the separator without MOFs (1032, 1025, 989 and $925~\mathrm{{mA}~h~g^{-1}},$ ), respectively (Fig. 14e). So as a modifier for separator, the packing density of MOF particles as well as a proper electrochemical window (Fig. 14f) of MOFs and electrolyte is crucial to obtain a respectable battery performance. Stephan et al. found that the polysulfides were effectively obstructed by the Mn-BTC-based separator on account of the repulsive ionic forces generated from the $\mathbf{COO^{-}}$ in the Mn-BTC [166].
## 3.3  COFs for Lithium–Sulfur Batteries
COFs, a class of covalent crystalline porous solids with permanent nanopores and lightweight elements, could potentially act as the host material in LSBs. Similar to MOFs,
COFs also can trap sulfur and soluble polysulfides in their pores/channels to diminish the shuttle effect.
Wang et al. firstly proposed a sulfur cathode for LSBs by using COFs as host materials [167]. They synthesized 2D COFs, CTF-1 (covalent triazine-based frameworks), which has the nanoporous structure constructed by the stacking of 2D layers (Fig. 15a). Later in 2016 and 2018, Wang reported two kinds of COFs, 2D porphyrin-based COF (Por-COF) [168] and 2D pyrene-based COF (Py-COF) [169], as the host material (Fig. 15c). CTF-1, Pro-COF and Py-COF showed surface areas of 789, 1095 and $2093~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The total pore volume and pore size of these three COFs also presented the same trend of gradual increase just as the surface area. Coincidentally, according to the thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) test, the sulfur loading densities in COFs also monotonically increased as the surface area and the pore volume enlarged, which were 34, 55 and $70\mathrm{\wt}\%$ , respectively. The Py-COF/S battery even achieved a reversible capacity of $481.2\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}}$ after 500 cycles at $5.0\mathrm{C}$ owing to the fast ion transport in the pores of Py-COF. These three systematic works proved that a well-designed 2D COF could be applied as a competent host for LSBs.
Lee et  al. established a “microporous COF-net on mesoporous CNT-net hybrid architecture” to trap polysulfides in LSBs [170]. Two COFs with different pore sizes (COF- $1{=}0.7~\mathrm{nm}$ , COF- $.5{=}2.7~\mathrm{nm}$ ) were stably coated on the CNT-net (Fig. 15d) through $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction. By means of DFT calculation, the adsorption energies $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ of polysulfides on the COFs and CNT can be resolved into the following three parts: “bond formation $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{bond}})$ , reconstruction of the host structure $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{recon}})$ and structural change of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ guests $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x}})$ .” First, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ $(x>2)$ molecules built Li–C bonds on the CNT surface without structure changes. $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ molecules formed a bidentate binding and anchored on the CNT surface (Fig. 15e). Next, after adsorption of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ , COF-5 performed stabilized structure and exhibited higher adsorption energies $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ than CNTs as well as great capability for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ capture. Based on the simulation results, the long-chain $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S_{x}$ molecules are difficult to be implanted into COF-1 due to the size effect. And the higher bond energy between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ and COF-1 $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{bond}}=-5.28\mathrm{eV})$ proved that it is favorable to form chemical bonds between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ and COF-1. Then, it is verified that the S in $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ and the B in COF-1 have strong interaction and also generate chemical bonds. These computation results explained why COF-1 is suitable for capturing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm S$ .
## 3.4  Summary
In summary, the biggest stumbling block of LSBs is the “shuttle effect”; therefore, employing CPMs as host materials and separators are both powerful schemes to restrain the effect (Table 3). For host materials, (1) the nanoscale cavities and cages of CPMs can effectively suppress the diffusion of polysulfides into the electrolyte. (2) The open metal centers and specific functional groups can provide Lewis acid–base interactions and bonding effect to retard the migration of soluble polysulfides out of the pores. (3) Smaller particle size of CPMs and hybridization with conductive materials (i.e., conductive polymer and CNT) are able to advance the overall conductivity, accelerate the internal transport of $\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}$ and shorten the ion diffusion pathway. (4) Large surface area and pore volume of CPMs can lead to high sulfur loading in CPMs/S composites. Hence, the materials that incorporate these characteristics (small particle size, large surface area, abundant functional sites and high conductivity) are appreciable to achieve a high-performance LSBs. For separators, CPMs generally act as ionic sieves that selectively pass ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ while restrain the diffusion of polysulfides to the anode side. CPMs also contribute to blocking polysulfides and enhancing the utilization of active materials as well as inhibiting the growth of lithium dendrites during cycling. Therefore, it is imperative to exploit more well-designed CPMs in LSBs to reach higher battery performance (Fig. 16).
## 4  Lithium–Air Batteries
Rechargeable lithium–air batteries (LABs) have recently attracted substantial attention for their high theoretical gravimetric energy density $(3505~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}}$ [54] based on discharge product $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ . Typically, an aprotic lithium–air battery is comprised of a lithium metal anode, a porous gas-diffusing cathode and organic electrolyte as $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ conductor. In recent years, $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ selective membranes or protectors are developed as important components in LABs. This is because apart from pure oxygen, the presence of nitrogen, carbon dioxide and water vapors in the air can lead to complex side reactions deteriorating the LABs system in capacity, efficiency and longevity. Consequently, many obstacles need to be overcome before considering practical applications, such as developing effective catalysts, suppressing the formation of lithium carbonate and blocking the moisture from the ambient air.
MOFs have shown great potentials in electrocatalysts and separators for $\mathrm{Li-air/O_{2}/C O_{2}}$ batteries, owing to their outstanding adsorbability to gas molecules, tunable pore size, open metal centers and well-defined crystal structures.
In 2014, for the first time, pristine MOFs were utilized as cathode materials for $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. Li et al. [171] investigated the battery performance of five MOFs, MOF-5, HKUST-1 and M-MOF-74 series $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ , Mn, Co), respectively. Their study indicated that MOFs with open metal sites enhance the ${\bf O}_{2}$ concentration (at least eighteen times higher than gaseous ${\bf O}_{2}$ under ambient pressure) within the pores taking advantage of their capability of binding $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ molecules (Fig. 17a, c). Among them, MOFs with open metal sites (HKUST-1 and M-MOF-74) who show higher $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ uptake exhibited greatly improved discharge capacity in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery test. In particular, the discharge capacity of MnMOF-74-based cell achieved $9420\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $50\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under 1 atm of oxygen, which is the highest among all the MOF-based cathodes (Fig. 17b). On the contrary, the capacity of the cell without MOFs $(2170\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) was much less than the best performer. The authors also synthesized a material, $\mathrm{{\bfMn}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ (dobdc $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2{,}5$ -dioxido-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate), by covering the open metal sites of Mn-MOF-74, the cell based on which displays a discharge capacity of $2820\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ , that is only $30\%$ of the capacity obtained from Mn-MOF-74. This result proved the contribution of open metal sites in MOFs to high capacity $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. Ever since, there are growing research interests on MOF-based lithium–oxygen batteries. Beyond the effects of open metal sites, Li’s group [172] further reported that the particle size of Co-MOF-74 can also exert an influence on its electrochemical behavior. Their work revealed that the enhanced battery performance ( $11,350\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at 100 mA $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ ) of Co-MOF-74 is owing to its higher electrical conductivity and more efficient diffusion process. In addition to Mn- and Co-MOF-74, bimetallic MnCo-MOF-74 has been investigated as well [173]. Benefitting from the synergistic integration of Mn- and Co-metal clusters, MnCoMOF-74-based cathode showed high full discharge capacity of $11,150\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ and good reversibility of 44 cycles (capacity limit of $1000\mathrm{mAhg^{-1})}$ , outperforming the monometallic MOF-74 counterparts.
A Ni-MOF with micro–nano structure, Ni( $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy) $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC)}$ $(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bipy}=4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}$ -bipyridine; $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid), was reported by Chen et al. [174] in 2015, which showed a remarkable performance as rechargeable $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ electrodes. It was found that the distinguishing features of this Ni-MOF 3D micro–nano structure, open catalytic sites and large specific surface area make them capable of guaranteeing the free transfer of ${\bf O}_{2}$ and effective contact between the electrolyte and the catalytic sites (Fig. 17d). The improvement of the Ni-MOF was evidenced by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and cycling test at fixed capacity. Specifically, the Ni-MOF exhibited more positive ORR $2.84\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}$ ) and more negative OER $3.84\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/$ Li) potential compared with conductive carbon (Cabot Vulcan XC-72), and the discharge–charge overpotential was slashed in cycling test (fixed capacity of $600\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.12\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) (Fig. 17e). The reversibility of MOF-based cell was also revealed by SEM analysis: the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was firstly formed inside the nanopores of Ni( $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipy) $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bf{BTC})}$ to produce nanosized products, therefore providing more sufficient $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ –electrolyte interfaces and complete decomposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Furthermore, the Ni-MOF-based cell presented a high capacity of $9000{\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}}$ , a high round-trip efficiency of $80\%$ and a respectable cycling of 170 cycles without obvious voltage drop (Fig. 17h). When assembled into a pouch-type Li– ${\bf\mathcal{O}}_{2}$ battery, it achieved an energy density of almost $500\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , showing extreme potential in energy storage industry.
MOFs also play another crucial role in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. Apart from developing high-performance cathode, an additional effective strategy to improve the reversibility of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries is constructing electrolyte with mediators to promote the efficiency of ORR and OER on the cathode. DBBQ $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{TBBQ}=2,5$ -di-tert-butyl-1,4-benzoquinone) and $\mathrm{TTF^{+}}$ ( ${\mathrm{TTF}}=$ tetrathiafulvalene) are effective redox mediators for
ORR and OER in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries, respectively. However, the strong reducibility of both DBBQ and $\mathrm{TTF^{+}}$ can lead to Li degradation because of redox shuttle, deteriorating the cycling stability of $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ batteries. Zhou et al. [175] designed a flexible separator based on $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})}_{2}$ (HKUST1) with 3D tailored porosity by in situ growing MOFs on commercialized Celgard separator. Acting as a ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ sieve in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries, the HKUST- $1@$ Celgard separator with uniform pore size prevented the migration of redox mediator to lithium anode (Fig. 18a). Compared with 22 cycles of normal glassfiber separator, the assembled cell with MOFbased separator obtained longer cycle life of 100 cycles in capacity-limit cycling test $(5000\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}, $ at a high current density of $\mathrm{1~A~g^{-1}}$ (Fig. 18e, f). The MOF-based separator here can maximize the benefits of the dual mediator strategy during both discharge and charge processes, accelerate the decomposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ film and inhibit the redox shuttle efficiently.
Considering the fact that $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries are far more limited since it can be impractical to use batteries in pure $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , developing Li–air system working in the ambient air is urgently needed. However, the existence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the air can lead to the formation of a notorious insulating discharge side-product, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , on the cathode. Compared with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ is more stable and more indecomposable $\lbrack\Delta_{\mathrm{f}}G^{\mathrm{o}}(\mathrm{Li}_{2}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{3}){=}{-}1132.1\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , $\Delta_{\mathrm{f}}G^{\mathrm{o}}(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}){=}{-}578.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}]$ [176–179]. Thus, in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , the charge overpotential is consequently increased, further threatening the cyclability of Li–air batteries. Therefore, it is significant to develop efficient catalysts to decompose the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ . Against this backdrop, $_{\mathrm{Li-CO}_{2}}$ battery with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ as the main discharge product is an ideal platform for investigating the influence of catalysts on the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ formation and decomposition. Besides, the potential of application of $_{\mathrm{Li-CO}_{2}}$ batteries under $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -concentrated conditions such as power plants and Mars exploration also leads to wide attraction.
It is recently found that MOFs show promising potential when they work as cathode catalysts in $_{\mathrm{Li-CO}_{2}}$ batteries. In 2018, our group [55] demonstrated for the first time that MOFs are beneficial to the enrichment and capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as well as the decomposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ as porous cathode catalysts in $\mathrm{Li\mathrm{-}C O}_{2}$ batteries. Not only low charging overpotential, but also high discharge capacity can be realized in MOF-based Li– $\mathbf{\cdotCO}_{2}$ cells with the help of catalytic metal sites separated in highly porous frameworks (Fig. 17a). We selected eight porous MOFs $(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , Mn(bdc), Fe(bdc), $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{bdc}})$ , $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{N}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{HCOO})_{2})$ and non-porous $\mathbf{MnCO}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MnO}$ to investigate the effects of different metal centers and pore structures on $_{\mathrm{Li-CO}_{2}}$ battery performances (Fig.  19b). The results revealed that $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOFs outperformed other candidates: $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ with larger pores delivered a discharge capacity as high as $18,022\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 19e), and $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{HCOO})_{2}$ with smaller pores provided a low charge overpotential of $\sim4.0\:\mathrm{V}$ even at $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles, showing that $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{II})$ centers are effective in capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules during discharge and catalyzing the decomposition of discharge products during charge. Isosteric heats of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ reveals that MOFs with a moderate $\boldsymbol{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ value can achieve smaller charge polarization at high current density due to the weaker interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Fig. 19d). It is also proved that the morphology of discharge products deposited on MOF-based electrodes tends to be nanostructured with a uniform dispersion in the whole electrode, which activates the inert $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ to guarantee a facile and complete decomposition upon charge.
Even though the irreversible decomposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ could be assuaged by high-efficient catalysts to perform relatively low charge potential in Li–air batteries, there are still other important factors such as humidity restricting the batteries’ cycle life. Therefore, it is also imperative to find proper gas-selective membranes to effectively avoid the unwanted components in complex ambient environment.
Lu et al. [180] reported a kind of mixed matrix membrane (MMM) using Al-based MOF (CAU-1 $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ ) incorporated with polydopamine (PDA) and polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA) (Fig. 20a). A thin layer of polydopamine was firstly coated on the surface of CAU-1- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ particles to form CAU$\mathrm{1-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA}$ ; then, the composite was mixed with PMMA in a certain ratio to prepare the MMM. Not only the robust membrane exhibited good thermal and chemical stability, but also the functional groups (including - ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ in MOFs, –OH in PDA and $-C=0$ in PMMA) helped to capture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ successfully and prevented the corrosion of anode caused by moisture, which obviously improve the cycling performance of Li–air batteries in the existence of $30\%$ RH. In addition, the $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ battery was assembled by using MMM as the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -selective membrane and the cycle number of coin cell with MMM was significantly improved compared to that of the cell without MMM or only CAU- $\boldsymbol{1-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with PMMA (Fig. 20b–e). Gathering the advantages of CAU- $\boldsymbol{1-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , PDA and PMMA together, MMM possesses high oxygen permeability, superb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbability and extremely strong hydrophobic properties simultaneously, which all lead to a durable Li–air battery.
To summarize, efforts have to be exerted to clear laborious hurdles in the field of lithium–air batteries: (1) developing gas-selective membranes which enable rapid diffusion of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and prohibit the penetration of unwanted air components to avoid Li corrosion, (2) designing cathode catalysts to reduce overpotential efficiently and help the discharge products to decompose favorably and completely, (3) devising separators or electrolytes to restrain the growth of dendritic lithium and SEI in the anode of lithium metal so as to increase the cyclability. Accordingly, owing to the desirable features of high specific surface area, abundant open catalytic sites and diverse pore structures, MOFs have enormous potentials in the application in lithium–air batteries. (1) As gas/ion-sieving membranes: as a semi-open system, LABs need to confront intricate circumstance in open air. Membranes that incorporate MOFs own well-defined pores and designable functional groups, which can screen oxygen or other gas components in a controllable way. Besides, it can improve the reversibility of the cell and decrease the possibility of side reactions. (2) As chemical catalysts of cathodes: the huge variety of MOFs catalysts is invaluable for understanding the relationship between materials’ structure and the performance of lithium–air batteries. In particular, Mn-based MOFs that showed outstanding behavior in both $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and Li– $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ batteries are worth in-depth investigation in LABs to maximize their potential in real application. (3) As ion-transporting electrolyte/separators, MOFs possess unique and tunable tunnel structures, which would be advantageous to prevent the rapid growth of lithium dendrites and guarantee a favorable ionic transportation.
## 5  Supercapacitors
Supercapacitors have attracted great attentions among all energy storage technologies due to their high power density and distinctive long cyclic stability which could compensate for rechargeable batteries. According to the charge storage mechanism, supercapacitors can be categorized either as electrochemical double-layer capacitors (EDLCs) or pseudocapacitors. On the one hand, porous carbon materials with high surface area have been used for commercial EDLCs and typically perform stable charge/discharge cycles, while
EDLCs are limited in low areal/volumetric capacitance and low areal/volumetric power density. On the other hand, transition metal oxides and conductive polymers are usually utilized in pseudocapacitors through providing fast and reversible redox reactions, but the drawback is relatively poor cycling [181–184].
CPMs, including MOFs and COFs, which have ultra-high specific surface area, adjustable porosity and versatile active units, have been investigated as alternative candidates for all kinds of electrochemical applications [12, 30, 185, 186]. More specifically, in supercapacitor, according to the charge storage mechanism, the application of pristine CPMs can be classified in two ways: using pristine CPMs to provide high surface areas in EDLCs and utilizing pristine CPMs to deliver reversible redox reactions in pseudocapacitors.
## 5.1  Pristine CPMs in EDLCs
The initial pristine MOFs used directly as electrode materials for EDLC was Co8-MOF-5 [187], $\mathrm{Zn}_{3.68}\mathrm{Co}_{0.32}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , which showed typical EDLC behavior with high specific surface area of $2900\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . More research of the relationship between MOFs structures (i.e., morphology, pore size and surface area) and their supercapacitor performances was reported later [188, 189].
Although MOFs possess large surface area, it is not easy to allow fast diffusion of electrons in MOF skeletons because of the non-conductive property of MOFs. Downsizing MOFs to nanocrystals is a feasible approach to solve the poor electrical conductivity. A series of 23 different nanocrystalline MOFs with varied metal units, organic linkers and structural topologies was investigated by Yaghi et al. [190]. In particular, a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF (Fig. 21c), named nMOF-867 $\mathrm{(Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(B P Y D C)_{6}}$ , $\mathrm{BPYDC}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}.}$ dicarboxylate), exhibited the best performance among other MOFs with stack capacitance of $0.64\mathrm{Fcm}^{-3}$ and areal capacitance of $5.09\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Besides, the capacitance of nMOF-867 was well sustained over 10,000 cycles. In addition to scrutinizing the relationship between MOFs’ properties and their performances, this is the first coin-shaped device of pristine MOF-based supercapacitors (Fig. 21a).
Compositing with conductive materials (such as conductive polymer and carbon materials) is another promising way to enhance the electrical conductivity. The first flexible solid-state supercapacitor based on MOFs was presented by our group [191]. Wang et al. interwove ZIF-67 crystals with electrochemically deposited polyaniline (PANI) on the conductive substrate (Fig. 21d, e), carbon cloth (denoted as PANI-ZIF-67-CC). PANI-ZIF-67-CC, which combined high surface area of MOFs with high conductivity of PANI, showed outstanding area capacitance of $2146\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ at the scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, a symmetric flexible solid-state supercapacitor was fabricated and proved the potential for wearable electronics.
Later, other conductive materials have been developed thoroughly. Xu et al. [192], for example, fabricated ZIF67-PPy $(\mathrm{PP}\mathrm{y}= $ polypyrrole) network for flexible supercapacitor. The electrode achieved high areal capacitance of $225.8~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Almost at the same time, Hosseinian et al. [193] synthesized the nanocomposite of ZIF-67 and reduced graphene oxide that showed great cyclic stability with $80\%$ retention after 1000 cycles at $\mathrm{~1~A~g^{-1}~}$ . Ce-MOF/GO and Ce-MOF/CNT were also reported by Ramachandran et al. [194]. Owing to the abundant mesoporous structure and the oxygen functional groups in Ce-MOF/GO, the capacitive performance achieved $2221.2\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $\mathrm{1~A~g^{-1}}$ . Thereafter, Lan et al. [195] introduced PPy into a polyoxometalate-based MOF (NENU-5) and the resulted NENU-5/PPy nanocomposite presented improved specific capacitance $\mathrm{5147~mF~cm^{-2}},$ ) than the pristine MOF. Moreover, a symmetric supercapacitor was assembled and exhibited high areal capacitance $1879\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2},$ ). Yang et al. [196] designed and synthesized Ni-MOF/CNT ( $\mathrm{CNT=}$ carbon nanotube) as the positive electrode material which exhibited capacitive performance of $1765\mathrm{Fg^{-1}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ . And the asymmetric supercapacitor device constructed with reduced graphene oxide/graphitic carbon nitride $({\mathrm{rGO}}/{\mathrm{g}}.$ - $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ ) delivered high energy density $(36.6~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}})$ and power density $(480\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1},$ ). With the emerging of various types of supercapacitor, a solid-state fabric fiber supercapacitor based on a MOF/conductive polymer hybrid (UiO-66/ polypyrrole) was fabricated [197]. This fibrous supercapacitor showed outstanding electrochemical performance and promising potential in wearable textile electronics.
Recently, the exploration of conductive 2D MOFs has becoming a thriving field. Dincă et al. [198] proved a conductive Ni-MOF $(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{HITP}{=}2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexaiminotriphenylene) as the electrode material (Fig.  22a, b) without using any other additives for EDLCs. A symmetric supercapacitor cell was also built up. Similar to carbon materials, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ exhibits both high surface area $(630~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ ) and superb electrical conductivity (over $5000\ \mathrm{S\m^{-1}},$ ). What’s more, the cell shows very high area normalized capacitance and volumetric capacitance as well as excellent stability with less than $10\%$ capacitance loss after 10,000 cycles.
In situ fabrication of MOF materials into nanowires on current collector is another effective strategy. Xu et al. [199] proved that the conductive MOFs nanowire array ( $\mathrm{Cu}$ -CAT NWAs) grew on carbon fiber papers displayed superior performance (Fig. 22c–f). Compared with crystalline powder synthesized via solvothermal method, Cu-CAT NWAs showed some specific properties, such as greatly improved conductivity and more effective charge transfer process due to the binder-free fabrication. Moreover, a solid-state supercapacitor with high areal capacitance and good rate performance was constructed.
## 5.2  Pristine CPMs in pseudocapacitors
The first MOF reported as the electrode material in pseudocapacitors was Co-MOF-71 [200]. The mixture of CoMOF-71 and polyethylene glycol (PEG) was bladed on conductive ITO glass substrate first, and then, the PEG was decomposed by annealing at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The Co-MOF film displayed pseudocapacitors performance with specific mass capacitance up to $206.76\mathrm{Fg^{-1}}$ . However, Marken et al.
[201] had an argument about the stability of Co-MOF-71 in electrolyte by the evidence of discoloring and CV results. Afterward, with different redox-active ligands and metal centers, zinc, cadmium [202] and iron-based MOFs [203] were employed as the electrode materials and showed pseudocapacitive performances accordingly.
Besides, owing to the unique morphology which could help achieve successful charge transfer and electronic transport, 2D layered CPMs also showed potential as pseudocapacitor electrode materials. The first attempt to utilize 2D MOF as the active material for supercapacitors was achieved by Wei et al. [204]. A Ni-based MOF with layered structure (Fig. 23a) was investigated, and the specific capacitances were 1127 and $668\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of 0.5 and $10\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , respectively. In their further study [205], Zn-doped Ni-MOF (Fig. 23b) showed higher capacitance of 1620 and $854\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of 0.25 and $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Subsequently, two layered structure Co-MOFs [206, 207] displayed improved capacitances of 2474 and $2564\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathbf{1}\operatorname{A}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition to nanosheet morphology, the accordion-like Ni-MOF superstructure was synthesized and examined as pseudocapacitive materials. An asymmetric flexible solid-state supercapacitor established by accordion-like Ni-MOF (Fig. 23c) and activated carbons was also proved to be a high-performance device [208]. Beyond 2D MOF-based energy storage devices, nickel-based MOF nanorods were synthesized and exhibited pseudocapacitive behavior as well [209]. The constructed Ni-based MOF nanorods/graphene asymmetric supercapacitor achieved
$166\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathbf{1}\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Lately, mixed-metal MOFs are proved to have better electrochemical performance than the corresponding monometallic MOFs [210, 211].
Consistently, 2D COFs with open channels for electron transfer and redox-active sites are also hopeful materials for pseudocapacitors [212, 213]. In an early study [214], Dichtel et al. demonstrated that a $\beta$ -ketoenamine-linked 2D COF (DAAQ-TFP-COF, $\mathrm{DAAQ}=2{,}6$ -diaminoanthraquinone, $\mathrm{TFP}=1,3,5$ -triformylphloroglucinol) exhibited reversible redox activity based on its anthraquinone subunits (Fig. 24a), chemical stability in acidic electrolyte and high surface area. Their research revealed the promise of using 2D COFs for supercapacitor. In their following work [215], the same COF was grown on Au working electrode to form an oriented thin film (Fig. 24c) that exhibited an order of magnitude improvement in areal capacitance than growth of the DAAQ-TFP COF as an oriented thin film on Au electrodes, morphology that provides superior electrochemical performance with increased capacitance and enhanced energy and power densities. Reprinted with permission from Refs. [214, 215]
the initial randomly oriented powder as a consequence of exposing more redox-active anthraquinone moieties. This work shows the importance of controlling morphology for capacitive performance.
Owing to the crystallinity and the predetermined structure of COFs, atomic control of the capacitive behavior could be achieved through tuning the functionality of COFs’ skeletons. Jiang et al. [216] developed a general approach to convert electrochemically inactive iminelinked Ni-porphyrin COFs (named NiP-COFs) into redoxactive COFs by post-synthetic functionalization with 4-azido-2,2,6,6-tetramethyl-1-piperidinyloxy (TEMPO). By varying the density of TEMPO radicals on the channel walls (Fig. 25a), a higher capacitance of $167\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was achieved in $\mathrm{[TEMPO]_{100\%}}$ -NiP-COF at the current density of $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . Banerjee et al. [217] integrated redox-active hydroquinone moieties on a stable 2D COF and reported their pseudocapacitive performances. A remarkable specific capacitance of $416\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ at the current density of $0.5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was achieved due to the proton-coupled electron transfer of hydroquinone/benzoquinone moieties assisted by the H-bonding with neighboring amine groups on the COF backbone.
Recently, Bao et al. [218] reported two 2D conductive hexaaminobenzene (HAB)-derived MOFs (Ni-HAB and CuHAB) which have superb chemical stability in aqueous electrolytes and reversible redox behaviors. Due to ample redox centers in HAB and the dense structure of these MOFs, high areal capacitances over $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ and exceptionally high volumetric capacitances up to $760\ \mathrm{F\cm^{-3}}$ were reached based on the results from submillimeter-thick MOFs pellets (Fig. 26a). What’s more, Ni-HAB showed high cycling stability that the capacitance of Ni-HAB sustained $90\%$ of initial value after 12,000 cycles at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . Later, Han et al. [219] investigated the pseudocapacitance of another 2D conductive MOF (Ni-CAT, $\mathbf{CAT=c}$ atecholate). NiCAT nanorods and layered double hydroxide (NiCo-LDH) nanosheets were grown on Ni foam (Fig. 27a) and formed a hierarchical nanoarray material together that showed high areal capacitance of $3200\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ at $1\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . Besides, the inclusion of conductive materials with pristine CPMs also can solve the problem of the poor electrical conductivity of crystalline porous materials. Active carbon [220], graphene [221], carbon nanotubes [222] and PEDOT (poly(3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene)) [223] were employed to enhance the electron conductivity and specific capacitance of redoxactive MOFs and COFs.
Above all, CPMs have exhibited promising potential as electrode materials in both EDLCs and pseudocapacitors (Table 4). Compared with conventional carbon materials employed in EDLCs, CPMs not only own large electrolyte-accessible surface area but also have molecular-level adjustable structure and tailorable channels/pores. In pseudocapacitors, the structural diversity and tunability as well as the functional versatility in CPMs also brought bright prospect. Moreover, the emerging conductive CPMs and the rapidly developing strategies that can enhance the inferior conductivity are gradually covering the shortage of CPMs in electrochemical fields. Combining high specific surface areas with intrinsic redox-active units, CPMs are therefore one of the most promising candidates in energy storage supercapacitors.
## 6  Other Batteries
Except Li-based batteries and supercapacitors, CPMs also have been exploited in other kinds of batteries, such as Naion battery (PBAs [224], MIL-100(Fe) [225], Co-HAB [226]), K-ion battery $\mathrm{(L{\mathrm{-}C o_{2}(O H)_{2}B D C}}$ [227], MIL-125(Ti) [228]), dual-ion battery $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ [229]), redox flow battery (RFB, $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC [230] and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -UiO-66 [231]).
Choi et al. reported a Prussian blue analogues (PBA)- based materials, named as sodium zinc hexacyanoferrate (NZH), $\mathrm{Na_{2}Z n_{3}[F e(C N)_{6}]_{2}{\cdot}\Delta x H_{2}O}$ , which could provide sites for Na ions to diffuse during charge/discharge process [224]. The initial reversible capacity is about $56.4~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , which is very closely to its theoretical capacity, $64.8\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The XRD patterns showed no change after 50 cycles, indicating that the NZH is stable enough over cycling. Bao et al. successfully designed a 2D MOF, named Co-HAB, with excellent conductivity, numerous redoxactive sites, and high chemical/structural stability for Na-ion storage [226]. It was proved that the high specific capacity
$(291\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) was contributed by the reversible storage of 3 electrons and $1\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ per HAB in electrolytes.
MIL-125(Ti) was firstly applied in K-ion battery in 2017 by Feng et al. and exhibited excellent capacity retention and coulombic efficiency over 2000 cycles at $200\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ [228]. The high reversible capacity was mostly due to the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ storage mechanism: $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ directly inserted into organic linkers without destroying Ti sites. Fe(dobpdc) showed a potential for dual-ion battery [229]. It remained stable for more than 50 cycles and exhibited a maximum specific energy of $316\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}$ . Yu et al. designed a MOF-based membrane for non-aqueous RFB by in situ growth $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC in a Celgard separator [230]. The RFB with $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC-based membrane exhibited excellent discharge capacity ( $94\%$ of theoretical value) and capacity decay rate $0.09\%$ per cycle). As expected, CPMs also manifested outstanding performances in these batteries (Table 5).
## 7  Conclusions and Perspectives
Pristine crystalline porous materials including metal–organic frameworks and covalent organic frameworks are emerging as a singular class of porous solids which offer large surface area, tunable pore/channels, various redox centers as well as well-defined structure. We have reviewed the most recent advancements of pristine CPMs in the electrochemical energy storage systems which include rechargeable batteries (lithium-ion batteries, lithium–sulfur batteries, lithium–air batteries) and supercapacitors. Overall, CPMs predominantly exhibit preeminent performance in three aspects: electrode materials, electrocatalysts and electrolyte/separator materials. For electrode materials, extraordinary surface area of CPMs plays the key role. For example, sulfur in Li–S batteries, gas in Li–air batteries and charge in EDLCs can be either adsorbed or enriched by CPMs. Besides, the controllable porosity and active sites provide ample facilities to deposit and decompose the generated products, respectively, suppressing electrodes’ volume expansion during the charge/ discharge process. For electrocatalysts, open metal sites and functional ligands in MOFs as well as redox organic building blocks in COFs primarily contribute to the superior performance. The diversity of metal species and variety of organic units create tremendous opportunities for developing multifunctional electrocatalysts. Finally, CPMs are able to achieve fast transport of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ and diversified sieving in electrolytes/ separators due to their unique tunable functionality and designable structure on the molecular level. More importantly, except for direct contributing to EES systems, CPMs could be employed as ideal platforms for in-depth study of the structure–activity relationship underneath which is hard to realize in conventional amorphous porous materials. Due to the complexity and elaborateness of CMPs, apparently more detailed characterization and thorough investigation, such as in situ study, are highly demanded to reach full understanding. Meanwhile, theoretical analysis and computational simulation should also be utilized to explore more deeply.
However, the poor electrical conductivity is still the “Achilles’ heel” of CPMs in electrochemical energy storage systems. To address this challenge, various strategies have been proposed. First, particular morphologies, such as nanowires and nanosheets, could shorten the electron transport pathway and thus ameliorate the conductivity. Second, designing novel CPMs with 2D layered structure is another new direction. What’s more, the exfoliation of 2D CPMs could further bring to the beneficial nanosheets morphology. Next, introducing conductive materials, such as carbonaceous species and conductive polymers, into CPMs to form composites is also a feasible strategy. Finally, developing conductive CPMs is the most straightforward tactic to break the bottleneck. In addition to the insufficient electrical conductivity, the stability of CPMs upon cycling is also worth-noticing. In general, COFs display satisfactory stability based on the reported studies. However, the cycling performance of MOFs is relatively poor. There are several approaches to strengthen. MOFs based on high valence metal ions such as Al and $Z\mathrm{r}$ usually exhibit excellent stability; introducing specific functional groups such as—F on the ligand to change the steric hindrance and electronic effects—can improve the stability of MOFs during cycling;
exfoliation of CPMs into 2D layer nanosheets also exhibits some certain feasibility for enhancing the electrochemical stability. These strategies have achieved gratifying progress which makes CPMs even more appealing for practical applications.
By reviewing the recent advancement, we notice an interesting trend that on the basis of materials, more and more researchers pay attention to the design and optimization of the device. Not only the activity of the material has been reported, the performance of the EES device has also been characterized, especially in supercapacitors. Various symmetric and asymmetric supercapacitors were fabricated, and their performances were also used to indicate the properties of materials although sometimes it is not as accurate as expected due to the inevitable gap between materials and devices. Nevertheless, this trend reflects the concern about practical application, and these efforts are informative for further exploration.
To summarize, in terms of next generation of electrochemical energy storage systems, CPMs are one class of the most promising materials that play a key role in all aspects. They can either transport ions or conduct electrons, and can be employed as the electrode materials, electrocatalysts and electrolytes/separators. With effective strategies compensating for the conductivity, the development of electrochemical energy storage systems will be certainly promoted by the pristine crystalline porous materials vigorously in the foreseeable future.
